window.SEED = [{"path":"concepts/articles.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [grammar]\n---\n\n# Articles (a / an / the)\n\nEnglish requires articles before most singular countable nouns. Korean has no articles, so learners frequently omit them (\"I sent email\" → \"I sent an email\").\n\nRules of thumb: `a/an` for first mention or non-specific, `the` for shared/specific reference (\"the attached file\").\n\nOften surfaces alongside [[present-perfect]] accuracy issues under speaking pressure.\n"},{"path":"concepts/business-email-tone.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [writing]\n---\n\n# Business Email Tone\n\nDirect grammatical correctness can still read as blunt (\"Send the file now.\"). Softeners make requests polite without being wordy.\n\nUseful patterns: \"Could you...\", \"When you get a chance...\", \"I'd appreciate it if...\", \"Just following up on...\".\n\nKorean learners often need [[articles]] attention in emails too. Phrasal verbs (see [[phrasal-verbs]]) keep the tone efficient.\n"},{"path":"concepts/conditionals.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [grammar]\n---\n\n# Conditionals\n\nType 0 (general truth), Type 1 (real future: `if + present, will + base`), Type 2 (unreal present: `if + past, would + base`), Type 3 (unreal past).\n\nMost common error: putting \"will\" in the if-clause (\"If I will study\" → \"If I study\"). This backshift rule is the core hurdle.\n\nOften drilled together with [[relative-clauses]] for exam readiness.\n"},{"path":"concepts/listening.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [listening]\n---\n\n# Listening Comprehension\n\nThe skill of following spoken English in real time. Stamina matters: many learners track short clips but lose detail past 3-4 sentences.\n\nCommon causes: unknown vocabulary mid-passage, fast connected speech, and not \"chunking\" meaning.\n\nBuild with graded clips, summary practice, and targeted vocabulary. Strong listening underpins exam sections and natural conversation.\n"},{"path":"concepts/phrasal-verbs.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [vocabulary]\n---\n\n# Phrasal Verbs\n\nVerb + particle combinations whose meaning isn't literal (\"circle back\", \"loop in\", \"flesh out\", \"run by\", \"follow up\").\n\nHeavy in workplace English; mastering them is a fast route to sounding natural and avoiding over-explanation.\n\nPairs well with idiom study and supports [[business-email-tone]] in professional writing.\n"},{"path":"concepts/present-perfect.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [grammar]\n---\n\n# Present Perfect\n\nForm: `have/has + past participle`. Used for past actions with present relevance, life experience, and unfinished time periods (\"since 2019\", \"for three years\").\n\nCommon Korean-L1 error: using simple past where present perfect is required (\"I work here since 2019\" → \"I have worked here since 2019\").\n\nRelated: [[articles]], and contrasts with the simple past in narrative. See also conditional backshift under [[conditionals]].\n"},{"path":"concepts/r-l-distinction.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [pronunciation]\n---\n\n# R / L Distinction\n\n/r/ and /l/ are distinct phonemes in English but map to similar sounds in Korean, causing merges (\"collect\" vs \"correct\", \"rice\" vs \"lice\").\n\n/l/: tongue tip touches the ridge behind the teeth. /r/: tongue pulls back, touching nothing.\n\nOften the slower-to-fix of the two main pronunciation targets, the other being the [[th-sound]]. Drill with minimal pairs and slow-then-fast shadowing.\n"},{"path":"concepts/relative-clauses.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [grammar]\n---\n\n# Relative Clauses\n\nClauses that modify a noun using who/which/that/whose. Defining clauses (no commas) vs non-defining (with commas, use \"which\" not \"that\").\n\nKey rule: the relative pronoun can be dropped when it's the OBJECT, but not when it's the SUBJECT (\"the man that called\" — can't omit \"that\").\n\nFrequently paired in exam prep with [[conditionals]].\n"},{"path":"concepts/th-sound.md","content":"---\ntype: concept\ntags: [pronunciation]\n---\n\n# The \"th\" Sound (/θ/ and /ð/)\n\nTwo sounds spelled \"th\": voiceless /θ/ (\"think\") and voiced /ð/ (\"this\"). The tongue lightly touches the upper teeth.\n\nCommon substitution for Korean L1: /s/ for /θ/ (\"think\" → \"sink\") and /d/ for /ð/ (\"this\" → \"dis\").\n\nTricky in clusters like \"months\". A frequent target alongside the [[r-l-distinction]] when isolating broad pronunciation issues.\n"},{"path":"README.md","content":"# 영어 튜터 지식 베이스 (Vault)\n\n이 볼트는 1:1 영어 교육 튜터의 실제 수업 기록을 시뮬레이션한 샘플 데이터입니다. \"아메바 시스템\"이 학생의 특성을 시간에 따라 수렴(convergence)하고 분기(divergence)하는 것을 테스트하기 위해 만들어졌습니다.\n\n## 구성\n\n- `students/` — 학생 3명의 수업 노트\n  - `Jiwon Park/` — 중급 성인, 비즈니스 영어 목표 (세션 6개)\n  - `Minseo Lee/` — 십대, 시험/문법 중심 (세션 6개)\n  - `Daniel Cho/` — 고급, 유창성/발음 + 관용표현 (세션 6개)\n- `concepts/` — 문법/발음 개념 참고 노트 (위키링크로 상호 연결)\n\n## 노트 형식\n\n각 세션 노트는 YAML frontmatter로 시작합니다: `student`, `session`, `date`, `category`, `tags`, `strength`. `strength`는 해당 관찰이 얼마나 강하게 성립하는지를 나타내는 0.0~1.0 실수값입니다.\n\n## 아메바 동작 메모\n\n- **수렴 예시**: Jiwon의 초기 시제 실수(현재완료, 과거시제 혼동)가 후반 세션에서 하나의 \"tense-aspect\" 패턴으로 요약됩니다.\n- **분기 예시**: Daniel의 초기 \"발음 문제\" 노트가 후반에 [[th-sound]]와 [[r-l-distinction]]으로 분리됩니다.\n"},{"path":"students/Daniel Cho/session-01.md","content":"---\nstudent: Daniel Cho\nsession: 1\ndate: 2026-01-15\ncategory: goals\ntags: [fluency, pronunciation, intro]\nstrength: 0.7\n---\n\nFirst session with [[Daniel Cho]], 29, software engineer who lived in Canada for 2 years. Already fluent and confident.\nGoal: sound less \"textbook,\" more natural — wants idioms, [[phrasal-verbs]], and to fix lingering accent issues that bug him in meetings.\nSpeaking is fast and accurate grammatically. Main thing holding him back is pronunciation, but it's hard to localize on day one — noted broadly as \"pronunciation problems.\"\nAlso tends to over-explain; could tighten his answers.\nHomework: record a 2-minute self-intro, send the audio.\n"},{"path":"students/Daniel Cho/session-02.md","content":"---\nstudent: Daniel Cho\nsession: 2\ndate: 2026-01-22\ncategory: pronunciation\ntags: [pronunciation, th-sound, errors]\nstrength: 0.6\n---\n\nListened back to his audio. The broad \"pronunciation problems\" note is splitting into specific targets.\nFirst clear one: the [[th-sound]]. He substitutes /s/ and /d/ — \"think\" → \"sink\", \"this\" → \"dis\". Very consistent, so trainable.\nIdiom work was fun: taught \"on the same page\" and \"ballpark figure,\" he used both correctly within minutes.\nOver-explaining still present but he's aware of it now.\nHomework: minimal pairs list for th vs s/d, record 10 words.\n"},{"path":"students/Daniel Cho/session-03.md","content":"---\nstudent: Daniel Cho\nsession: 3\ndate: 2026-01-29\ncategory: pronunciation\ntags: [pronunciation, r-l-distinction, errors]\nstrength: 0.55\n---\n\nSecond pronunciation target isolated today: the [[r-l-distinction]]. Separate from the [[th-sound]] issue — \"collect\" vs \"correct\" merge for him.\nSo the original single \"pronunciation problems\" node has now diverged into two clean sub-issues (th, and r/l). Good for tracking progress independently.\nth-sound homework paid off: \"think\" is now correct most of the time, \"this/that\" still slips.\nIdioms: \"cut corners,\" \"back to the drawing board.\" Strong retention.\nHomework: r/l minimal pairs (rice/lice, fry/fly), plus use 2 new idioms in a Slack message at work.\n"},{"path":"students/Daniel Cho/session-04.md","content":"---\nstudent: Daniel Cho\nsession: 4\ndate: 2026-02-05\ncategory: vocabulary\ntags: [phrasal-verbs, idioms, fluency]\nstrength: 0.7\n---\n\nIdiom and [[phrasal-verbs]] deep-dive. Worked on work-meeting phrasals: \"circle back,\" \"loop in,\" \"flesh out,\" \"run by.\"\nHe absorbs these fast and the over-explaining habit is shrinking as he replaces long sentences with tighter phrasal verbs.\nPronunciation maintenance: [[th-sound]] now ~90% accurate; [[r-l-distinction]] is the slower one, still inconsistent under fast speech.\nHe mentioned he loves basketball — great source for natural idioms (\"slam dunk,\" \"full-court press\").\nHomework: write a meeting recap email using 4 of today's phrasal verbs.\n"},{"path":"students/Daniel Cho/session-05.md","content":"---\nstudent: Daniel Cho\nsession: 5\ndate: 2026-02-12\ncategory: pronunciation\ntags: [r-l-distinction, pronunciation, fluency]\nstrength: 0.65\n---\n\nFocused session on [[r-l-distinction]] since it's his slowest-improving area. Drilled tongue position; used \"really\" / \"lily\" contrast.\nProgress is real but fragile — accurate when slow, slips when excited or speaking fast in basketball talk.\n[[th-sound]] is essentially solved now; only \"th\" in clusters (e.g. \"months\") trips him.\nFluency overall is excellent; the polish is now in fine phonetic detail rather than communication.\nHomework: shadow a 1-minute podcast clip focusing only on r and l sounds.\n"},{"path":"students/Daniel Cho/session-06.md","content":"---\nstudent: Daniel Cho\nsession: 6\ndate: 2026-02-19\ncategory: pronunciation\ntags: [pronunciation, th-sound, r-l-distinction, idioms]\nstrength: 0.8\n---\n\nCheckpoint for [[Daniel Cho]]. The day-one \"pronunciation problems\" blob has fully diverged and resolved into two tracked threads: [[th-sound]] (solved except clusters) and [[r-l-distinction]] (good when not rushing).\nIdioms and [[phrasal-verbs]] have converged into a natural, automatic part of his speech — he no longer over-explains.\nRemaining work is narrow: r/l under fast speech, and \"th\" in consonant clusters. Both are polish, not problems.\nHe sounds noticeably more native in mock meetings; the textbook stiffness is gone.\nPlan: maintenance drills + advanced register (formal vs casual switching).\n"},{"path":"students/Jiwon Park/session-01.md","content":"---\nstudent: Jiwon Park\nsession: 1\ndate: 2026-03-04\ncategory: goals\ntags: [business-english, intro, fluency]\nstrength: 0.7\n---\n\nFirst session with [[Jiwon Park]]. Works in supply-chain planning at a mid-size electronics firm, needs English for weekly calls with overseas vendors.\nMain stated goal: sound \"professional and not rushed\" on conference calls. Currently over-prepares scripts and freezes when asked unexpected questions.\nSpeaking is hesitant but vocabulary is solid for her domain. Comprehension is strong.\nNoticed she avoids the present perfect entirely, defaulting to simple past even when describing ongoing situations (\"I work here since 2019\").\nHomework: write 5 sentences about current projects using \"have/has + been\".\n"},{"path":"students/Jiwon Park/session-02.md","content":"---\nstudent: Jiwon Park\nsession: 2\ndate: 2026-03-11\ncategory: grammar\ntags: [present-perfect, tense, errors]\nstrength: 0.55\n---\n\nReviewed [[present-perfect]] homework. She gets the form right when writing but reverts to simple past when speaking under time pressure.\nRecurring error: \"I didn't finish the report yet\" instead of \"haven't finished\". Marked this as a pattern to watch.\nAlso a separate-looking mistake today: dropped articles in \"I sent email to vendor\" — likely [[articles]] gap, common for Korean L1.\nBreakthrough moment: she self-corrected \"since 2019\" sentence without prompting. Good sign.\nHomework: read a short vendor email aloud, focus on natural rhythm.\n"},{"path":"students/Jiwon Park/session-03.md","content":"---\nstudent: Jiwon Park\nsession: 3\ndate: 2026-03-18\ncategory: errors\ntags: [tense, articles, errors]\nstrength: 0.6\n---\n\nTwo error families keep surfacing together. Tense: mixes past and present perfect (\"we already discussed this last meeting and we have not decided\").\nArticles: omits \"the/a\" before vendor/report/meeting nouns. These looked unrelated at first but both spike when she's nervous on calls.\nTried a role-play of an unexpected question — she froze for 4 seconds, then recovered. Confidence is the real bottleneck, not knowledge.\nInterest discovered: she's into hiking, lit up describing a Bukhansan trip. Good topic for low-pressure warm-ups.\nHomework: 3-minute voice memo describing last weekend, no script.\n"},{"path":"students/Jiwon Park/session-04.md","content":"---\nstudent: Jiwon Park\nsession: 4\ndate: 2026-03-25\ncategory: writing\ntags: [business-email-tone, articles, writing]\nstrength: 0.65\n---\n\nFocused on email writing using [[business-email-tone]]. Her emails are grammatically clean but read as blunt (\"Send the file now.\").\nWorked on softeners: \"Could you\", \"When you get a chance\", \"I'd appreciate it if\". She adopted these quickly.\n[[articles]] error persists in writing too — \"Please check attached file\" → \"the attached file\". Same gap as speaking.\nVoice memo homework was excellent: relaxed, fewer freezes, used present perfect correctly twice unprompted.\nHomework: rewrite two of her real work emails with softer tone.\n"},{"path":"students/Jiwon Park/session-05.md","content":"---\nstudent: Jiwon Park\nsession: 5\ndate: 2026-04-01\ncategory: confidence\ntags: [fluency, confidence, business-english]\nstrength: 0.7\n---\n\nBig confidence jump. Did a 10-minute mock conference call with unexpected questions — only one freeze, much shorter recovery.\nShe's internalizing that hesitation is fine; native speakers pause too. Reframing helped more than any drill.\nTense errors now rare in relaxed speech but still appear when stressed — confirms earlier hypothesis that it's anxiety-linked, not a knowledge gap.\nUsed [[business-email-tone]] softeners verbally too (\"when you get a chance\") which sounded natural.\nHomework: lead the warm-up next session, pick the topic (suggested hiking).\n"},{"path":"students/Jiwon Park/session-06.md","content":"---\nstudent: Jiwon Park\nsession: 6\ndate: 2026-04-08\ncategory: grammar\ntags: [tense, present-perfect, articles, fluency]\nstrength: 0.8\n---\n\nSummary checkpoint for [[Jiwon Park]]. The two early error families (tense confusion + [[articles]] omission) have converged into one clear profile: both are accuracy-under-pressure issues, not gaps in understanding.\nPresent perfect is now reliable in writing and in calm speech; only stressed speech triggers regressions.\nArticles remain the most persistent surface error but no longer block communication.\nConfidence is the headline win — she led the hiking warm-up fluently and handled three curveball questions.\nPlan: shift focus to advanced negotiation phrasing next month. Keep light article drills as maintenance.\n"},{"path":"students/Minseo Lee/session-01.md","content":"---\nstudent: Minseo Lee\nsession: 1\ndate: 2026-02-20\ncategory: goals\ntags: [exam-prep, grammar, intro]\nstrength: 0.75\n---\n\nIntake session with [[Minseo Lee]], 16, high school sophomore. Target: school midterm/finals and eventually 수능 English.\nMotivated but exam-anxious. Strong at memorized vocabulary, weak at applying grammar rules under timed conditions.\nInitial diagnostic: trouble with [[relative-clauses]] and conditionals on multiple-choice items.\nAlso makes broad \"verb tense\" mistakes that I couldn't pin down yet — will observe over next sessions.\nHomework: 10 multiple-choice grammar items, mark which ones she guessed.\n"},{"path":"students/Minseo Lee/session-02.md","content":"---\nstudent: Minseo Lee\nsession: 2\ndate: 2026-02-27\ncategory: grammar\ntags: [relative-clauses, grammar, errors]\nstrength: 0.6\n---\n\nDrilled [[relative-clauses]]. She confuses \"which\" vs \"that\" and drops the relative pronoun where it can't be omitted.\nThe broad \"verb tense\" issue from session 1 is starting to split into two distinct things: (1) tense agreement in reported speech, (2) conditional tense backshift (\"if I will go\").\nGot faster on items she understood — speed problem is partly confidence, partly rule gaps.\nLikes K-drama; we used a subtitle line to explain a relative clause and it clicked instantly.\nHomework: rewrite 8 sentences combining two clauses with who/which/that.\n"},{"path":"students/Minseo Lee/session-03.md","content":"---\nstudent: Minseo Lee\nsession: 3\ndate: 2026-03-06\ncategory: grammar\ntags: [conditionals, tense, errors]\nstrength: 0.65\n---\n\nFocused on [[conditionals]]. Confirmed the split from the vague \"verb tense\" note: her real weakness is conditional backshift, not tenses in general.\nClassic error: \"If I will study hard, I will pass\" — fixed the will-in-the-if-clause rule with a memorable contrast pair.\nSecond conditional (\"If I were you\") felt unnatural to her; drilled with K-drama scenarios she enjoyed.\nReported speech tense agreement is improving separately — these two really are different problems now.\nHomework: 6 conditional transformation items, type 1 and type 2 only.\n"},{"path":"students/Minseo Lee/session-04.md","content":"---\nstudent: Minseo Lee\nsession: 4\ndate: 2026-03-13\ncategory: errors\ntags: [relative-clauses, conditionals, exam-prep]\nstrength: 0.7\n---\n\nMixed review of [[relative-clauses]] and [[conditionals]] under exam timing. Accuracy up to ~80% on relatives, ~70% on conditionals.\nRemaining relative-clause issue narrowed to ONE thing: omitting \"that\" when it's the subject (can't be dropped). Clean, isolated error now.\nTest anxiety still spikes her error rate; she rushed and missed two items she actually knew.\nInterest note: wants to study media/communications in university — motivating angle for English.\nHomework: timed 15-item mixed quiz, simulate exam pressure.\n"},{"path":"students/Minseo Lee/session-05.md","content":"---\nstudent: Minseo Lee\nsession: 5\ndate: 2026-03-20\ncategory: confidence\ntags: [exam-prep, confidence, listening]\nstrength: 0.6\n---\n\nPost-midterm check-in. She scored 88 on the school English exam, up from low-70s — grammar section was her strongest part.\nConfidence noticeably higher; she said the conditional rules \"finally made sense.\"\nNew weakness surfaced now that grammar is solid: listening comprehension on longer passages. She loses the thread after 3-4 sentences.\nThis is a fresh branch to track, separate from her grammar work.\nHomework: one short [[listening]] clip, summarize in 3 Korean sentences.\n"},{"path":"students/Minseo Lee/session-06.md","content":"---\nstudent: Minseo Lee\nsession: 6\ndate: 2026-03-27\ncategory: listening\ntags: [listening, exam-prep, vocabulary]\nstrength: 0.55\n---\n\nCheckpoint for [[Minseo Lee]]. Grammar weaknesses have converged into a short, stable list: subject \"that\"-omission and occasional 2nd-conditional slips. Everything else is exam-ready.\nThe early vague \"verb tense\" cloud fully resolved into [[conditionals]] backshift + reported speech, now both handled.\nNew focus area is [[listening]]: she follows short clips but drops detail on longer ones, often because of unknown vocabulary mid-passage.\nPlan next month: build listening stamina + targeted vocab. Grammar shifts to light maintenance.\nHomework: daily 2-minute listening clip, note one new word each.\n"}];
